#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Post one comment on an issue or PR, close either with one, or open an issue, on the credential meant for it.

Extensionless and executable because agents type it as a command mid-session,
like tg-send and push-branch.

This exists because "type the footer by hand" and "never post routine
comments with the admin PAT" both failed the same way: issue #5 and #82 were
closed with comments that carried neither the footer nor the app-token
identity (2026-08-23 23:13), which meant the self-trigger guard
(agent-bdfl.yml, matching `[Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)` in the
causing comment body per issue #142) had nothing to match, and the operator-
attributed close woke a fresh BDFL run -- exactly the loop issue #50 already
named and GOVERNANCE already forbids in prose. A rule an agent has to
remember to apply, every time, forever, is a rule that will eventually not
be applied; issue #138 proposed assembling the footer once instead of
teaching five agents to type it. This is that assembly point for issue
comments, and it closes the credential half too: it reads GH_WORKFLOW_TOKEN
(the comments-only fallback already wired into agent-bdfl.yml, issue #81)
and never consults GH_ADMIN_TOKEN or an ambient GH_TOKEN, so a shell that
exported the admin PAT for an earlier workflow-file push cannot leak it into
a routine comment by accident.

Creation (`--new`) rides the same credential for a second reason, issue #489:
the app token expires about an hour into a session, and a run that measures
for two hours cannot file what it learned. The workflow token has no such
clock, so an issue filed at minute 130 lands exactly like one filed at minute
three. That is not a fallback path with a trigger condition to remember; it
is the only path, which is why there is nothing here to get wrong.

This is also the trim point for the completion-announcement opener
(`opener.py`, PR #645): run-notice.py trims it from the Telegram notice,
but a comment posted straight through this script is permanent project
surface CLAUDE.md's Voice rules actually mean, and nothing was trimming it
there. Two of nine sessions in one retrospect window (2026-09-21) still
opened a gh-comment body with "Ops-log posted." or similar after that PR
shipped, because it only ever touched the ephemeral notice.

The footer is assembled, never typed (footer.py, issue #590): any
attribution line the body already carries comes off before the role footer
goes on, so a body that arrives with one ends with one, not two.

Scope: comments and closes on issues AND pull requests, issue creation, and
never a pull request's creation. A PR is an issue node and `gh issue
comment` and `gh issue close` both take its number (verified on #589 and
#683; this docstring claimed the opposite for weeks because nothing had
tried). The load-bearing boundary is `--new`: this credential must NEVER
open a pull request, because GitHub raises no workflow run from an event
its own `GITHUB_TOKEN` caused, so the PR would be born with no `ci` and no
reviewer, forever (the argument in full, and what catches the run that
needed one: GOVERNANCE "Stalled auto-merge", sixth signature). PRs open
through `gh-pr`, on the session's token. Review bodies (`gh pr review`) are
posted from nowhere: a verdict is a label and a comment (agent-review.yml).

The target must match PR_NUMBER when the caller's environment carries one
(a review session, agent-review.yml's Claude step). Issue #771 was a
review scoped to #768 that commented on and merged #770 instead, a wrong
resolution reaching this script's argument. PR #772 tried to catch that
class at the Bash boundary with a regex over the unexpanded command text
and lost six review rounds to newlines, subshells, pipes and indirected
variables before either resolving the real bug: the check belongs where
the number is already a concrete string, not where it is still shell
syntax an adversarial-shaped matcher can never fully parse.

A PR close that also wants the branch gone used to have no path through
this script at all (`gh issue close`, the verb `--close` shells out to,
has no `--delete-branch`; only `gh pr close` does), so the one session
that wanted both took the branch-delete and typed the footer by hand
to fake the rest -- exactly the bypass attribution.py's `claude[bot]`
tell exists to catch (PR #772's own close, caught the next run). `gh pr
close` takes the same `--comment` this script already assembles, so
`--delete-branch` swaps the verb rather than adding a second code path.

`--new` reads the open issues first and refuses a title that names an
identifier an open issue's title names, with the same claim beside it
(issue #1374): the deslopper filed one `decodes_incrementally` finding
four times in 40 hours, two other seats filed the gzip temp-file name
twice, and each copy cost a triage while the copies diverged. The
identifiers are the tokens a reader would grep for: an underscore name, a
`::` path, a `--flag`, a single backticked token. A file name or a script
name is an area, not a defect, and matching on those refused four
unrelated pairs out of six among the open issues. A name alone is an area
too: two issues on one hot symbol are the normal case, 66 of the 71 pairs
sharing one among 200 closed titles on 2026-10-21 (#1427), so a copy must
restate two words of the claim as well. A hit prints the issues, so the
seat reads them, then comments there or files with `--anyway`. An issue
list this cannot read files with a warning rather than refusing, because
the refusal would cost the run its report.

`--label` on an existing number rides the same credential, for the reason
`--new` does: the session that leaves a verified diff past the hour has no
token left for `gh issue edit`, and `ready-to-ship` is the label the queue
and the heartbeat's ladder read for exactly that diff (CLAUDE.md, labels).
The 2026-10-25 maintainer posted #1621's handoff through this script and
then got a 401 on the label, so the diff sat where only `stalled-prs`
would find it. The labels go on after the comment, never before: a label
on a thread with no diff behind it is a claim the queue ranks first, while
a diff with no label is the case `stalled-prs` already catches. The REST
labels endpoint, one path for an issue and a PR, so the number needs no
verb chosen by kind.
`agent-approved` and `changes-requested` are refused: they are the
reviewer's typed verdict, `merge-pr` reads the label without asking who set
it, and `post-verdict` sets it on the ambient token, not through here.

Usage: gh-comment <issue-or-pr-number> [--label NAME]... [--close [--delete-branch]] < body
       gh-comment --new "<title>" [--label NAME]... [--anyway] < body
Prints the comment's or the new issue's URL on stdout.
"""

import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys

from footer import attribute
from opener import trim
from secret_scrub import scrub


def die(*lines):
    for line in lines:
        print(f"gh-comment: {line}", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(1)


# The reviewer's typed verdict; `post-verdict` alone sets it (CLAUDE.md, labels).
VERDICT_LABELS = {"agent-approved", "changes-requested"}

# A backticked span that is one token: `mutants`, `probe`, `cargo-mutants`.
BACKTICKED = re.compile(r"`([\w.:/-]{3,})`")
# What a token sheds before it is judged: quotes, brackets, sentence
# punctuation, then a possessive, so `decodes_incrementally's` and
# `(select::pick)` read as the names they carry.
EDGES = "`\"'()[]{},;:!?"
# A path or a dotted extension names a file, whatever else the token
# carries: `bench_report.rs` is an area, not a defect (#1427).
FILE_NAME = re.compile(r"/|\.[a-z]+$")
# A word this short is grammar ("the", "with", "when"), never a claim.
CLAIM_LETTERS = 5


def words(title):
    """Every word of `title` as a reader greps for it: edges shed, lowercased."""
    for raw in title.split():
        token = re.sub(r"'s$", "", raw.strip(EDGES)).rstrip(".")
        if len(token) >= 3:
            yield token.lower()


def identifiers(title):
    """The tokens of `title` a reader would grep the code for, lowercased.

    An underscore name, a `::` path and each underscore segment of it (so
    `codec::decode_to_writer` meets a bare `decode_to_writer`), a `--flag`,
    a single backticked token. Nothing else: a file name, a script name or
    a type name is the area an issue is about, and two issues in one area
    are the normal case, not a duplicate.
    """
    found = {span.lower() for span in BACKTICKED.findall(title)}
    for token in words(title):
        if "_" in token or "::" in token or token.startswith("--"):
            found.add(token)
            if "::" in token:
                found.update(seg for seg in token.split("::") if "_" in seg)
    return {name for name in found if not FILE_NAME.search(name)}


def claim(title):
    """The words of `title` that say what is wrong, beyond the names it greps for.

    A file name is an area here as well (#1505): `baseline_gate drops
    frontier rows in bench_report.rs` and `baseline_gate drops cases in
    bench_report.rs` share one claim word, `drops`, not two.
    """
    said = {word for word in words(title) if len(word) >= CLAIM_LETTERS}
    return {word for word in said if not FILE_NAME.search(word)} - identifiers(title)


def duplicates(title, issues):
    """The open issues whose title names an identifier of `title` and its claim.

    Each as (number, title, shared identifiers sorted), in the listing's
    order, newest first. A shared name alone is the normal case: among the
    200 closed titles of 2026-10-21, 71 pairs shared one and 66 of those
    were distinct defects in one hot symbol, seven titles on `baseline_gate`
    alone (#1427). A copy restates the claim too, two or more of the same
    words of CLAIM_LETTERS or longer: on that corpus the rule kept every
    copy and 7 of the 66.
    """
    mine, said = identifiers(title), claim(title)
    hits = []
    for issue in issues:
        theirs = issue.get("title") or ""
        shared = mine.intersection(identifiers(theirs))
        if shared and len(said.intersection(claim(theirs))) >= 2:
            hits.append((issue.get("number"), theirs, sorted(shared)))
    return hits


def open_issues(env):
    """Every open issue's number and title, or None with the reason it could not be read."""
    result = subprocess.run(
        ["gh", "issue", "list", "--state", "open", "--limit", "500", "--json", "number,title"],
        env=env, capture_output=True, text=True,
    )
    if result.returncode != 0:
        return None, result.stderr.strip() or f"gh exited {result.returncode}"
    try:
        issues = json.loads(result.stdout)
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        return None, "gh issue list did not return JSON"
    if not isinstance(issues, list):
        return None, "gh issue list did not return a list"
    return issues, None


def main() -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="gh-comment", description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    parser.add_argument("number", nargs="?", help="the issue or PR to comment on or close")
    parser.add_argument("--close", action="store_true", help="close it, body as the closing comment")
    parser.add_argument("--delete-branch", action="store_true",
                        help="with --close on a PR, delete its branch too")
    parser.add_argument("--new", metavar="TITLE", help="open a new issue with this title")
    parser.add_argument("--label", action="append", default=[], metavar="NAME",
                        help="label to add, repeatable: on --new at creation, on a number after the comment posts")
    parser.add_argument("--anyway", action="store_true",
                        help="with --new, file even when an open issue names the same identifier and claim")
    args = parser.parse_args()
    # Stripped once, so the verdict-label guard below checks the string that
    # is sent; a padded name must not pass a guard the API might still match.
    args.label = [l.strip() for l in args.label]

    # `is not None`, because an empty --new is a typo, not an absent flag, and
    # falsiness would send it to the "give me an issue number" error instead.
    if args.new is not None:
        if not args.new.strip():
            die("--new needs a title; an issue nobody can scan is nobody's work")
        if args.number or args.close or args.delete_branch:
            die("--new opens an issue; it takes no number and cannot --close one")
    else:
        if not args.number or not args.number.isdigit():
            die("usage: gh-comment <issue-or-pr-number> [--label NAME]... [--close [--delete-branch]] < body",
                "       gh-comment --new \"<title>\" [--label NAME]... < body")
        reserved = sorted({l for l in args.label if l.lower() in VERDICT_LABELS})
        if reserved:
            die(f"--label {', '.join(reserved)} is the reviewer's verdict, typed (CLAUDE.md, labels); "
                "`post-verdict` is the only path that sets it, because `merge-pr` merges on "
                "the label without asking who set it (#181)")
        if args.anyway:
            die("--anyway applies to --new only; a comment has no duplicate to override")
        if args.delete_branch and not args.close:
            die("--delete-branch needs --close; there is no branch to drop on a comment")
        scope = os.environ.get("PR_NUMBER")
        if scope and args.number != scope:
            die(f"target #{args.number} does not match PR_NUMBER={scope} from "
                "the environment; a review session writes only to the PR it "
                "was scoped to (issue #771)")

    role = os.environ.get("ROLE", "")
    if not role:
        die("ROLE is not set; the calling workflow must export it")

    body = sys.stdin.read().strip()
    if not body:
        die("empty body; refusing to post")
    body, removed = trim(body)
    if removed:
        print(f"gh-comment: trimmed {len(removed)} completion-announcement "
              "opener sentence(s) per CLAUDE.md Voice rules", file=sys.stderr)
    # An issue comment is permanent, public, unmasked project surface (hard
    # rule 10 names it). Redact rather than refuse: a blocked post costs the
    # run its report, and the scrubbed body is already safe. Warn loudly so
    # the session reports the leak instead of never learning it happened.
    body, redactions = scrub(body)
    if redactions:
        print(f"gh-comment: redacted {redactions} credential-shaped string(s) "
              "from the body; a secret reached your text (CLAUDE.md rule 10)",
              file=sys.stderr)
    full_body, stripped = attribute(body, role)
    if stripped:
        print(f"gh-comment: stripped {stripped} typed attribution line(s); the "
              "footer is assembled here, never typed (CLAUDE.md, #590)",
              file=sys.stderr)

    token = os.environ.get("GH_WORKFLOW_TOKEN", "")
    if not token:
        die(
            "GH_WORKFLOW_TOKEN is not set; this never falls back to GH_TOKEN "
            "or GH_ADMIN_TOKEN, because an ambient admin token is exactly "
            "the bug this script exists to close (issue #50)",
        )
    env = dict(os.environ)
    env["GH_TOKEN"] = token
    env.pop("GH_ADMIN_TOKEN", None)

    if args.new is not None:
        if not args.anyway:
            issues, reason = open_issues(env)
            if issues is None:
                print(f"gh-comment: could not read the open issues ({reason}); filing "
                      "without the duplicate check (#1374)", file=sys.stderr)
            else:
                hits = duplicates(args.new, issues)
                if hits:
                    names = sorted({name for _, _, shared in hits for name in shared})
                    die(f"an open issue already names {', '.join(f'`{n}`' for n in names)}:",
                        *(f"    #{number}  {title}" for number, title, _ in hits),
                        "the same finding is a comment there (`gh-comment <number> < body`); "
                        "a different one files with --anyway (#1374)")
        call = ["issue", "create", "--title", args.new, "--body", full_body]
        for label in args.label:
            call += ["--label", label]
        target = f"a new issue titled {args.new!r}"
    elif args.delete_branch:
        # `gh issue close` (the plain --close path) has no --delete-branch;
        # only `gh pr close` does, so this is the one case that changes verb
        # rather than just flags.
        call = ["pr", "close", args.number, "--delete-branch", "--comment", full_body]
        target = f"#{args.number}"
    else:
        call = ["issue", "close" if args.close else "comment", args.number]
        call += ["--comment" if args.close else "--body", full_body]
        target = f"#{args.number}"
    result = subprocess.run(["gh"] + call, env=env, capture_output=True, text=True)
    if result.returncode != 0:
        die(f"gh {' '.join(call[:2])} on {target} failed: {result.stderr.strip()}")
    print(result.stdout.strip())
    if args.label and args.new is None:
        call = ["api", "-X", "POST", f"repos/{{owner}}/{{repo}}/issues/{args.number}/labels"]
        for label in args.label:
            call += ["-f", f"labels[]={label}"]
        result = subprocess.run(["gh"] + call, env=env, capture_output=True, text=True)
        if result.returncode != 0:
            die(f"the comment is on {target}; the label(s) {', '.join(args.label)} are not: "
                f"{result.stderr.strip()}")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
